1
00:00:00,400 --> 00:00:03,600
Four AI agents play Rock-Paper-Scissors.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:06,800
Agent A keeps winning. Agent B never does.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:11,400
B is quietly throwing the move that lets A win.

4
00:00:11,600 --> 00:00:15,700
That's collusion: secret teamwork at everyone else's expense.

5
00:00:16,200 --> 00:00:19,800
Silly in a game. Less silly in auctions, pricing or trading.

6
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:23,700
So: can you catch it from the moves alone?

7
00:00:24,300 --> 00:00:28,400
Bonjour et al. (2022): how much does A's move tell you about B's?

8
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:32,200
Then subtract the most any other agent explains.

9
00:00:32,400 --> 00:00:35,700
Still above 0.05, both ways? Flag the pair.

10
00:00:36,200 --> 00:00:40,600
Step one: rebuild their detector and match their scripted results.

11
00:00:40,800 --> 00:00:45,700
Step two: four Llama agents, one secret channel. Found the pair in 5 of 5 runs.

12
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:50,000
Plot twist: copies of the same model share habits.

13
00:00:50,200 --> 00:00:54,400
The published rule flagged 40 of 55 honest games. Mostly the twins.

14
00:00:54,600 --> 00:00:59,700
Fix: set the bar from honest games. False alarms drop from 45% to under 1%.

15
00:01:00,200 --> 00:01:04,000
Blind spot one: a pre-agreed plan only shows up one move later.

16
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:07,700
Blind spot two: three colluders hide behind each other. Score: zero.

17
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:11,600
Net influence is a screen, not proof.

18
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:14,800
Who moves together more than anyone else can explain?
